---
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About Wiser Walk',
  url: new URL('/about/', Astro.site).href
};

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'About', url: '/about/' }
];
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="What Wiser Walk is, what it is not, and who is behind it."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <Hero title="About">
      Wiser Walk is a place to understand yourself a little better — through quizzes worth
      taking and articles worth reading.
  </Hero>
  <div class="prose">

    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      Self-assessments and short essays for Christians who would rather see themselves
      clearly than comfortably. Some of the quizzes are serious, some are lighter. Not all
      of them are about theology: some are about character, some about gifts, some about how
      well you actually know the Bible.
    </p>
    <p>
      The quizzes are the door. The articles are what is on the other side of it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Not a verdict on your faith.</strong> No quiz here scores anyone as a better or worse Christian.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a recruiting tool.</strong> The Compass tells you where you already stand; it does not argue you toward a position.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a church.</strong> Nothing here substitutes for one.</li>
      <li><strong>Not collecting your answers.</strong> No accounts, and no record of what you answered — every quiz is scored on your own device, so your answers never leave your browser.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a mailing list you get put on.</strong> There is one, and it is entirely opt-in: nothing is sent to it unless you type your address into the form and ask. If you never do, nothing about you leaves your browser at all. <a href="/method/#your-answers">What is sent, exactly</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>On fairness</h2>
    <p>
      The hardest part of a quiz like this is describing a position you disagree with in
      terms its holders would recognise. That is the standard everything here is held to,
      and the <a href="/method/">method page</a> shows the working — including the findings
      that were raised and deliberately not applied.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you hold a position and think it is described unfairly, that is worth hearing.
      Being wrong about someone else's belief is the one failure this site cannot afford.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
